Use case: illustration-story.
Asset type: ONE complete original landscape panorama for a sophisticated scene-building sticker game.
Theme: Alpine winter glass conservatory / 雪山玻璃温室.
Create a new horizontal 16:9 scene at the highest available native resolution, richly refined and complete. This is a grown-up scenic illustration with a variety of appealing natural places and exquisite useful objects, NOT a sticker sheet or toy world.

Scenery and composition: an elegant historic glass conservatory and timber chalet on a broad snowy mountain terrace, surrounded by distant blue-violet peaks and pale dawn clouds. The winter landscape should remain grand and spacious, not an ordinary packed room or a manor beside a summer lake. View from a slightly elevated continuous perspective. A warm honey-gold sun from the upper left catches snowy firs, amber window panes, weathered cream stone and a copper roof. The ENTIRE main conservatory has a legible complete outline within the canvas, including its curved roof crown, structural frame, base and attached terrace. Its glass makes several naturally connected inside/outside corners visible. A quiet snow path and distant valley create genuine negative space. Clear foreground, middle ground and atmosphere-reduced mountain background. Landscape, architectural space and plants dominate; no visual spotlight on the single person.

Drawing: refined hand-painted winter travel-book illustration with finely observed scenic realism, colored pencil edge accents, luminous but matte opaque watercolor/gouache glazing, delicate selective paper texture. Landscape may look realistically proportioned and highly detailed, with soft natural coordinated light and rich material distinctions. Preserve fine ornate details and crispness in meaningful objects instead of flattening all scenery. Ivory, snow blue, lavender mountains, fir green, orchid mauve, muted copper and honey light. Mature and elegant, no kawaii, no big-headed characters, no glossy 3D mobile game render, no artificial sparkle haze, no fake vintage yellow cast, no heavy outlines around everything.

Natural object-rich corners: 
- a greenhouse propagation bench with different orchid/camellia/hyacinth specimens, distinct bloom shapes and different growth stages, several terracotta and painted ceramic vessels of different height, garden scissors laid flat, seed box and watering vessel;
- a warm reading corner with one complete wooden chair, knitted throw draped coherently, small side table, closed illustrated books without readable text, a brass magnifier and a ceramic mug;
- a winter bouquet wrapping station with one open shallow crate, mixed evergreen branches and winter flowers, natural paper sheets, twine spool, ribbon and a finished wrapped bouquet placed on the table;
- a tiled low masonry stove with coherent closed firebox, firewood basket, copper kettle safely on a plate, shelf of glazed cups and spice tins;
- a compact telescope at a conservatory window with physically correct three-legged stand, all its feet on the floor, a closed map folio and brass compass on a grounded cabinet;
- a snow-facing outside timber deck with whole long bench, a closed antique travel case, a pair of boots next to a peg board with hanging coat/scarf, sled fully within the snow terrace, lantern with thick safe handle;
- raised greenhouse beds and planted stone islands with lush winter botanical shapes, a few tall sculptural leaves and flower clusters naturally offset. No rows of identical pots, no cloned flowers, no repetitive microscopic detailing or checkerboard specimen rack. Do NOT simply duplicate the tea-table + roses + iris + wicker-hat composition of another garden scene.

Arrange objects for real tasks and intriguing quiet clues, at varied scales, with coherent supporting surfaces and contact shadows. Around 8 naturally connected spatial corners. Aim toward enough distinct, meaningful complete groups for a future approximately 200–600 sticker design where readable; do NOT claim this with 200 tiny leaves, petals, sparkles or texture dots. Prefer complete large, medium and small objects that can eventually be detached and understood.

Person: EXACTLY ONE SMALL ADULT WOMAN inside the glasshouse, full body about 8% of canvas height, seen from behind looking toward the mountain window, with dark hair in a low bun, a cream knitted sweater and a deep rose calf-length skirt. She is visibly a simplified hand-painted nonrealistic figure with no skin microtexture; her facial features are not visible from this rear viewpoint. Normal adult anatomy, two arms relaxed at her sides and two grounded feet. No complex hand gestures, no anatomy collision with plant/table/door frames. Do not add other people or human figurines. Her unobscured silhouette is separate from furnishings.

Production discipline: all independent main objects, entire furniture, person, telescope, sled and containers stay at least 5% inside all image edges with complete feet and silhouettes. Continuous snow, terrain and sky may naturally extend to edges, but do not frame with giant cropped furniture or cropped ornate pillars. Roof/window/wood joinery physically continuous, consistent vanishing points and sound structural supports. Objects grounded, no melted handles, no fused flowers or endless disconnected bars, no floating boards, no cut-off heads or shoes. All landscape and scene lighting governed by one upper-left dawn sun; indoor stove may warm a small local corner without conflicting global cast shadows. Preserve shadow detail and print-friendly tonal/color separation, no pure black or blown white, no neon fluorescent palette. Avoid long thin burrs, extremely tiny filigree and uniformly noisy texture. No words, no signage, no seal, no watermark, no text on books, no collage panels or decorative border. One complete beautiful scenic painting; no blank base and no transparent-object sheet.

